          "text": "{\"translation_en\":null,\"larger_context_summary\":\"Jane speaks to Mr. Rochester, her employer and fiancé, shortly before their intended wedding at Thornfield. He has returned from a journey, and she insists on recounting without interruption the hopes and increasingly ominous impressions she experienced during his absence. Her confidence in their shared future is set against disturbing signs—including the changed weather, troubling dreams, and, later in her account, the mysterious woman who entered her room and tore the wedding veil. Jane does not yet know the decisive plot fact that Rochester’s wife, Bertha Mason, is alive and concealed at Thornfield, which makes her hopeful interpretation of marriage tragically incomplete.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The clause functions chiefly as a sincere report of an already existing feeling: introduced by “because,” it supplies Jane’s reason for welcoming the prospect of life with Rochester. Its place within an engagement gives it some relational consequence—hearing the avowal can reinforce intimacy and their anticipated union—but the words do not themselves promise marriage, create an obligation, or alter their status. Jane’s insistence that Rochester stop caressing her and let her speak underscores the deliberateness of the statement. Although the surrounding language is emotionally elevated, intensity alone does not make an utterance expressively reflexive; this is controlled explanation rather than an involuntary cry. The dramatic irony lies outside the clause’s semantic force: Jane speaks truthfully from limited knowledge while Rochester conceals the impediment to their marriage.\"}"
